From: prospero-win <prospero-win@invalid>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 13:00:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] win32u: Send the PS5 title's input from one thread at a time.

Every GUI thread's message wait calls the driver's ProcessEvents, and the
wake pipe sets QS_DRIVER on all their queues, so several threads take
events from the title's queue at once. Two events posted together, such
as the Ctrl and S of one key combination, could then reach the server in
either order: one thread took Ctrl, another took S and sent it first, and
the game saw S before Ctrl. Let one thread at a time take and send the
events; a thread that finds another one sending leaves the queue to it,
since that one takes every event, in order.
---
 dlls/win32u/ps5drv.c | 11 +++++++++++
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+)

diff --git a/dlls/win32u/ps5drv.c b/dlls/win32u/ps5drv.c
--- a/dlls/win32u/ps5drv.c
+++ b/dlls/win32u/ps5drv.c
@@ -738,6 +738,12 @@
     TRACE( "gamepad connected or disconnected (%u): WM_DEVICECHANGE\n", changes );
     NtUserPostMessage( HWND_BROADCAST, WM_DEVICECHANGE, DBT_DEVNODES_CHANGED, 0 );
 }
+
+/* Every GUI thread's message wait calls ProcessEvents, and several can run it at
+ * once. Events taken from the queue by two threads could reach the server in
+ * either order (Ctrl and S of one key combination as S, Ctrl), so one thread
+ * at a time takes and sends them. */
+static pthread_mutex_t input_lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;
 
 static BOOL ps5_ProcessEvents( DWORD mask )
 {
@@ -770,6 +776,9 @@
             polled = TRUE;
         }
     }
+    /* A thread that finds another sending leaves the queue to it: that one
+     * takes every event, in order. */
+    if (pthread_mutex_trylock( &input_lock )) goto done;
     while (processed < PS5_INPUT_BATCH && next_input( &event ))
     {
         INPUT input = {.type = INPUT_KEYBOARD};
@@ -796,6 +805,8 @@
             break;
         }
     }
+    pthread_mutex_unlock( &input_lock );
+done:
     notify_pad_changes();
     /* The wake pipe is every GUI thread's queue fd, so a byte sets QS_DRIVER
      * on all their queues while one of them drains it and takes the events;
